LETTERBOX on

TITLE open_signal_logo

SHOT
A held shot on a transmitter mast at dusk, red light blinking, nobody underneath it. It has been blinking without instruction for eleven years. Nobody has ever gone up to ask it why.
SHOT_END

NARRATOR:
There is a period the Basin does not put in its histories, because the people who lived it are still alive and still embarrassed.

NARRATOR:
For eight years after the Handoff, the towers stayed up and nobody owned them. No licence. No authority. No signal police. Just a mast, a generator, and whoever climbed up there first.

NARRATOR:
And what went up those masts, night after night, to every set still working in the Basin, was filth. Everybody watched it. Nobody has ever admitted to it. That is the period, and those are the people, and this is what it cost them.

NARRATOR:
Everybody in this picture is a real person. The names have been changed to protect them from their families, and the families from the tape.

FADE out 3

ACT 1: The Open Signal
SLUG THE MERIDIAN ROADHOUSE, EAST BASIN | 2079, SUMMER

The Meridian is a roadhouse built into the shell of a truck depot. Two hundred people, one working ceiling fan, and a stage the size of a card table.

Behind the bar, MAURICE TAM runs the room the way a man runs a boiler he does not fully understand.

Out back, in the wash pit, a nineteen-year-old named DENNY FAUST scrapes plates for nine credits an hour.

At the end of the bar, in a coat too good for the room, a big man is watching the kitchen door. This is AUGGIE PRINE.

AUGGIE:
Maurice. The boy carrying the crates.

MAURICE:
That’s Denny. He does the plates. Don’t.

AUGGIE:
Don’t what?

MAURICE:
Whatever it is you do. I know what you do. Everybody knows what you do.

AUGGIE:
Then you know I do it politely.

MAURICE:
You do it politely and they never come back to the plates. That’s two dishwashers you’ve had off me.

Auggie Prine turns on his stool so that he is facing the room, which is what he does instead of arguing.

AUGGIE:
Maurice, why does a nineteen-year-old dishwasher get talked about in a bar he isn’t drinking in?

MAURICE:
He doesn’t.

AUGGIE:
He does. I have been in this room four times and I have heard about that boy from three different women who do not know each other, and one of them was crying laughing, and one of them was not laughing at all.

MAURICE:
It’s a small district.

AUGGIE:
It is a small district and I am a patient man and I would like you to say the thing out loud so that we can both stop performing.

Maurice Tam wipes a glass that is already clean, which is what a man does when he has decided to say it.

MAURICE:
He’s built wrong. That’s the polite version and it’s the only version you’re getting from me.

AUGGIE:
Built wrong.

MAURICE:
Auggie, the boy is nineteen years old and he is the most talked-about thing in East Basin and he does not know it yet, and I would like it very much if that lasted another year.

AUGGIE:
It will not last another year. It was never going to last another year. That is not me, that is arithmetic.

Auggie finishes his drink and does not order another, which in this bar is a kind of announcement.

AUGGIE:
Two dishwashers, you said. Where are they now?

MAURICE:
One of them has a house.

AUGGIE:
And the other?

MAURICE:
The other one has a house too, Auggie. That’s what makes it a hard conversation to win.

WAIT 4

SLUG THE MERIDIAN, THE LOT OUT BACK | LATER

The lot behind the roadhouse. A generator, a stack of crates, one working floodlight, and a truck with a camera rig bolted where a passenger seat should be.

DENNY:
Maurice says you’re a director.

AUGGIE:
Maurice says a lot of things and about a third of them are true, which is a very good average out here.

DENNY:
You do the tapes. The adult ones.

AUGGIE:
I do the adult ones.

DENNY:
My mother says the tapes are why the world ended.

AUGGIE:
Your mother is being generous. The world ended for reasons far above my budget.

Auggie leans against the truck. He does not come any closer, which he has learned to do on purpose.

AUGGIE:
Denny, I’m going to be direct, because the polite version of this conversation takes forty minutes and insults us both. I have heard about you.

DENNY:
Heard what.

AUGGIE:
I think you know what. I think you have known since you were about fifteen and I think nobody has ever said it to your face in daylight, and I would like to be the first, because after me it will be everybody.

Denny Faust stands in a floodlit lot behind a roadhouse holding a crate he has forgotten he is holding.

DENNY:
People talk.

AUGGIE:
People talk about a lot of things. They do not usually agree.

DENNY:
What do you want.

AUGGIE:
I want to look at you. That’s all it is tonight. You put the crate down, you show me, I say yes or no, and either way you have a job in the morning and nobody in that bar ever hears about it from me.

DENNY:
And if you say yes?

AUGGIE:
Then in one year you are the most famous man in the Basin and your mother stops speaking to you. I have watched that happen four times and I am not going to pretend it doesn’t.

There is a long moment in a floodlit lot. Somewhere inside the roadhouse a glass breaks and a room cheers about it.

Denny Faust puts the crate down.

The film does not show us. The film shows us Auggie Prine’s face, in a lot, in bad light, and Auggie Prine has been looking at people professionally for eleven years and his face does something it has not done in all of them.

AUGGIE:
Well.

DENNY:
Yeah.

AUGGIE:
No. I mean... well. Son, do you have any idea?

DENNY:
I’ve got some idea.

AUGGIE:
You have no idea. You have an idea the size of East Basin. I am telling you about the size of the world.

Denny picks his crate back up, because he does not know what else to do with his hands.

DENNY:
What is it you’d actually want me to do?

AUGGIE:
Work. Hit a mark. Do it eleven times when it stops being interesting on the second. Do it with lights on you and eight people watching who are all thinking about lunch.

DENNY:
And people watch that.

AUGGIE:
People watch anything. That is the tragedy and the business model. What I want is for them to watch it to the END.

AUGGIE:
Anybody can hold an audience for four minutes. Any idiot with a fire can do four minutes. I want to hold them for ninety.

DENNY:
Why ninety?

AUGGIE:
Because at ninety minutes they’ve given you their evening. And a person who gives you their evening in this world has given you the only thing they still own.

Denny looks at the rig. He touches the lens housing the way people touch things they have only seen on a shelf.

DENNY:
My shift ends at four.

AUGGIE:
Quit at four.

DENNY:
It’s a job.

AUGGIE:
It is a sink. I have been where you are standing. There is a sink in every life and the trick is noticing which one is yours.

FADE out 2

SLUG THE FAUST HOUSE, DRAY LANE | THAT NIGHT

A prefab unit with a dead lawn and a working porch light, which in this district makes it a mansion.

JOANIE FAUST has been waiting up in the dark, which is how you know it is not going to go well.

JOANIE:
Where were you.

DENNY:
Working. You know where I was.

JOANIE:
You were not working, because Maurice called here at eleven to ask where you were, so unless you were working somewhere ELSE.

DENNY:
I was talking to a man about a job.

JOANIE:
A man.

DENNY:
A director.

JOANIE:
A director. Say the rest of it, Denny. Say the part you’re trimming off the end.

DENNY:
He does the adult tapes.

JOANIE:
He does the adult tapes. And he came to a roadhouse to talk to a dishwasher.

DENNY:
It’s a job that pays in hard credits.

JOANIE:
Do you think I’m stupid? Do you think I have lived in this house with you for nineteen years and I am STUPID?

DENNY:
Mum...

JOANIE:
I do the washing, Denny. I have done the washing since you were four years old. There is not one thing about you I have not known for years and had the decency to say nothing about.

The kitchen is very small and there is nowhere in it to stand that is far enough away.

DENNY:
Then say it.

JOANIE:
No.

DENNY:
Say it, because everybody else in East Basin is saying it, and my own mother won’t, and I’m starting to think that’s the whole problem.

JOANIE:
You have got ONE thing. One. An accident. A thing that happened to you in the womb that you did not earn and cannot lose.

DENNY:
It’s something.

JOANIE:
It is nothing! It is a fact about your body! It is not a skill, it is not a trade, it is not a CHARACTER. You are a boy who washes plates and got dealt one card and you are about to sell it to a man in a coat.

DENNY:
He said I could be somebody.

JOANIE:
He said you could be LOOKED AT. Those are different words and he chose the one he chose because he has done this before.

DENNY:
At least he came to the house. At least somebody came.

JOANIE:
You’re nothing. Do you hear me? Under the one thing, you are nothing, and the world is going to find that out for you and it will not be gentle about the lesson.

The porch light hums. It is the only sound in the house for four seconds.

DENNY:
Then I’ll find out.

Denny takes a coat off the hook. He does not slam anything, which is somehow worse.

He walks. The porch light stays on behind him for a long time, because she does not turn it off, because turning it off would be admitting he is not coming back.

FADE out 3

SLUG THE PRINE HOUSE, ABOVE THE BASIN | THREE DAYS LATER

Auggie Prine does not own a studio. Auggie Prine owns a HOUSE, which is different, and which is the entire point of Auggie Prine.

The pool is drained and full of cable. The lounge is a set. The kitchen is a green room. Sixteen people live here who are not related to each other and would all say they are.

At the centre of it, on a lounger, holding court over a room she has never once needed to raise her voice in, is MARGUERITE SABLE.

SABLE:
You’re the boy from the roadhouse.

DENNY:
I’m Denny.

SABLE:
Not for long, sweetheart. Come sit down before you fall down. Have you eaten anything that wasn’t out of a machine this week?

DENNY:
I had soup.

SABLE:
That’s a no. Kestrel, get him a plate.

KESTREL rolls in from the kitchen. She is seventeen and she has a broadcast headset clamped over her ears that she does not take off, has not taken off, and will not take off in this entire picture.

KESTREL:
He can get his own plate. Is this the one Auggie’s been going on about?

SABLE:
This is the one.

KESTREL:
He’s been saying "wait till you see it" for three days like a man with a boat.

REED HALLORAN comes down the stairs with the specific confidence of a man who has never once been the smartest person in a room and has never once noticed.

REED:
Is this him? This is him. Hey. Reed Halloran. I’m the other one.

DENNY:
The other what?

REED:
The other guy in the scene. There’s always two guys. One of them’s the one they came for and one of them’s the one who makes the first one look taller. I’m very good at it.

SABLE:
Reed’s being modest. Reed can hit a mark in the dark and cry on the count of four.

REED:
I can. Watch. One. Two. Three.

Reed cries. It is instantaneous and completely convincing and he stops just as fast.

REED:
Four. See? Every time. It’s a gift and it has cost me two marriages.

WILBY CRANE is in the corner with a sound cart, headphones on, adjusting something that does not need adjusting. He does not look up.

WILBY:
Tell him to stand on the tape.

AUGGIE:
Denny, stand on the tape.

Denny stands on a strip of tape on the floor. It is the first mark he has ever hit. He will hit thousands.

AUGGIE:
Everybody. Come here a minute.

SABLE:
Auggie, the boy just walked in.

AUGGIE:
Marguerite, in this house we do not do the thing where everybody finds out one at a time in a corridor over four weeks. We do it once, we do it in daylight, and then it is over and he is a colleague.

He turns to Denny, and the enormous kindness of Auggie Prine is that he asks.

AUGGIE:
Is that alright with you?

DENNY:
Yeah. Yeah, okay.

Sixteen people in a lounge in a rented house look at a nineteen-year-old dishwasher, and the film gives us their faces and not him.

Reed Halloran laughs out loud, once, and then apologises, and then laughs again.

Marguerite Sable does not laugh. She looks at him for a long moment with something close to pity, because she is thirty-four and she knows exactly what a room full of that face costs.

Kestrel takes one hand off her plate and pushes her headset back a quarter inch, which for Kestrel is a standing ovation.

Wilby Crane says nothing at all, because Wilby Crane is a sound man and this is not his department.

REED:
Okay. Okay! Right. Okay. So... I’m the other one. I want that on the record. I’ve made my peace with it in the last four seconds.

SABLE:
Sit down, Reed.

AUGGIE:
Thank you, everybody. That’s the whole ceremony. We shoot Thursday.

The room goes back to being a room. Somebody puts something on. It takes about forty seconds and then it is genuinely, completely over, which is the single most decent thing that happens to Denny Faust in this entire picture.

SABLE:
He can’t work under Denny Faust. It sounds like a boy who mows lawns.

AUGGIE:
He’s not going to work under Denny Faust.

SABLE:
Then what.

AUGGIE:
Something with a stop in it. Something the ear catches. Two syllables and a hard letter.

Denny is standing on his mark in a stranger’s living room in a house above a dead city, being renamed by people who have known him for eleven minutes, and he does not stop it.

DENNY:
Vox.

AUGGIE:
What?

DENNY:
Vox. My mother had a hymn book. It was in the hymn book.

SABLE:
Vox. That’s good. That’s not bad at all. Vox what, though. You need a front half.

REED:
Something churchy. He’s got that face. He’s got a face like a boy who’s about to confess to something.

KESTREL:
Deacon.

Everyone turns and looks at the seventeen-year-old in the headset, who has not looked up from her plate.

KESTREL:
Deacon Vox. It sounds like a man on a poster. Can I go now.

AUGGIE:
Nobody move. Say it out loud, all of you. I want to hear it in a room.

SABLE:
Deacon Vox.

REED:
Deacon Vox!

WILBY:
Deacon Vox.

And there it is, in a rented house, in front of sixteen people, said aloud four times until it stopped being a joke and started being a name.

DEACON:
Deacon Vox.

FADE out 2

SLUG THE PRINE HOUSE, THE LOUNGE SET | THURSDAY

The first shoot. Three lamps, one camera, a bedsheet on a frame, and more concentration in the room than most governments manage in a year.

This is the part nobody outside this house understands. It is a workplace. There are marks on the floor and a schedule on the wall and a man with a clipboard worrying about the lamps.

AUGGIE:
Quiet. Wilby, speed.

WILBY:
Speed.

AUGGIE:
Scotty, boom’s in frame, come up two inches. Two. Two, Scotty.

SCOTTY KELL, nineteen, adores everyone in this room in a way that is going to cost him, and comes up two inches.

SCOTTY:
Sorry. Sorry, Auggie.

AUGGIE:
Don’t be sorry, be two inches higher. Marguerite, you’re on the couch. Deacon, you come in on the line.

SABLE:
What’s my read?

AUGGIE:
Tired. Not sad. Tired is more expensive than sad.

SABLE:
Tired I can do without acting.

Marguerite Sable takes her robe off and hands it to a woman whose entire job is to hold it, and sits down on a couch, and is at work.

SABLE:
Sweetheart. Look at me a second.

DEACON:
Yeah.

SABLE:
First one. Everybody’s first one is bad and nobody remembers it. Look at my face, not the lens, not Auggie, not the boom. My face is the whole job.

DEACON:
Okay.

SABLE:
And if it doesn’t happen, it doesn’t happen. It happens to everybody, it happened to Reed twice last month...

REED:
It was ONCE, Marguerite, and there was a wasp in the room.

SABLE:
And we go again after lunch and nobody says a word about it. Nobody in this house has ever said a word about it.

AUGGIE:
And ACTION.

Deacon Vox walks into frame in a stranger’s living room and says the first line he will ever say on tape.

DEACON:
You left the light on for me.

SABLE:
I always leave the light on.

DEACON:
The grid’s out three nights a week. That light costs you something.

SABLE:
Everything costs me something. The light I don’t mind.

And then they do the rest of it, and the film stays where it has been the whole scene, which is at the back of the room with the crew.

We watch Auggie Prine watch his monitor. We watch the clipboard man stop worrying about the lamps. We watch Scotty Kell’s arms shake holding the pole and not drop it.

We watch Wilby Crane, on the cart, take his headphones off, which Wilby Crane does not do, and look at the monitor like a man checking a number he has already checked.

Nobody in the room says anything for a long time after Auggie says cut. That is not a compliment. That is a room full of professionals doing arithmetic.

AUGGIE:
Cut. Print. Wilby, tell me.

WILBY:
I want to go outside for a minute.

AUGGIE:
Take your minute.

Wilby Crane goes outside and stands in a drained swimming pool full of cable and does not do anything in particular, because there is nothing in his trade to do about what he has just watched.

REED:
That was one take. That was ONE TAKE, Auggie.

AUGGIE:
I know what it was.

REED:
I’ve never done one take. I did nineteen takes of a man opening a door.

AUGGIE:
You were bad at opening the door, Reed.

REED:
I was! I was terrible at it! It’s a hard door!

Marguerite Sable puts the robe back on and lights something and does not say anything at all, and later she will tell Auggie that in eleven years she has never once been nervous on a couch and she was nervous on that couch.

KESTREL:
It’s going to be huge.

AUGGIE:
It’s going to be huge.

KESTREL:
No, I mean it’s going to be HUGE. I’m on the feed all night. There’s nothing out there. There is nothing out there but weather and lists.

FADE out 2

SLUG THE BASIN | THE FOLLOWING MONTH

A montage, which is a lie a film tells to skip the boring parts of getting rich.

A rented room in the Rust District. A woman at two in the morning, alone, with a set on. She has not moved in forty minutes.

A bar in Coldwater where the whole room turns to face the box, and a man who is losing at cards asks for quiet, and the barman does not change the channel even though there are women present, because the women asked him not to.

A checkpoint on the East Road where two militia stop arguing about a bribe because the guard-hut set is on and something is happening on it.

NARRATOR:
The tape went out on an unlicensed mast on a Tuesday. By Friday it had been recopied four hundred times by people with no equipment and worse ethics.

NARRATOR:
Nobody paid Auggie Prine for a single copy. That was not how it worked. It worked like weather.

NARRATOR:
What Auggie Prine got instead was the next one, financed by everyone in the Basin who wanted to be near it.

SLUG THE PRINE HOUSE, THE POOL | LATER

The Colonel has arrived. THE COLONEL is a soft man in a good coat who has never raised his voice because he has never had to.

COLONEL:
Auggie. I have watched your tape eleven times.

AUGGIE:
And?

COLONEL:
And I have not watched anything eleven times since the Handoff. I have not watched anything TWICE.

AUGGIE:
What are you offering, Colonel.

COLONEL:
Six thousand a picture. Tape stock. Two more lamps.

AUGGIE:
Ten. And I keep the negative.

COLONEL:
Nobody keeps the negative, Auggie, there is no law that says you keep the negative.

AUGGIE:
There is no law that says anything, which is why the man holding the negative is the man who owns it. Ten.

COLONEL:
Eight. And I want him in every one.

AUGGIE:
He’s in every one anyway. That wasn’t a concession, that was you describing the weather to me and calling it a deal.

The Colonel laughs. It is not a warm sound. Nothing about the Colonel is warm, and everybody in this picture will spend two years politely not noticing.

COLONEL:
I like you, Auggie. You’re the only man in the Basin still pretending this is a craft.

AUGGIE:
It IS a craft.

COLONEL:
It’s a hole in the world and everybody’s pouring themselves into it. But you make it look like a craft, and I’ll pay for the look.

WAIT 3

SLUG THE PRINE HOUSE, DEACON’S ROOM | THAT NIGHT

Deacon Vox has a room now. In it are a bed, a full-length mirror, and eleven hundred credits in a shoebox, which is more money than his mother has ever held at one time.

He stands in front of the mirror for a while. He is not vain about it yet. That comes later, and it comes fast, and it is the beginning of everything that goes wrong.

Right now he is nineteen and he is looking at himself the way you look at a lottery ticket you have not cashed.

DEACON:
Okay.

DEACON:
Okay. Okay.

Kestrel is in the doorway, headset on, watching him look at himself the way you watch a dog dream.

KESTREL:
You do that every night.

DEACON:
I’ve been here eight days.

KESTREL:
You’ve done it eight times. I’m very observant, it’s my whole personality, I don’t have any others.

DEACON:
What are you listening to? You’re always listening to something.

KESTREL:
Everything. All of it. Every channel that’s still up, all night, all at once.

DEACON:
Why?

KESTREL:
Because if I take it off it’s quiet, and when it’s quiet I can hear the house, and when I can hear the house I remember I live here on purpose.

DEACON:
Can I ask you something?

KESTREL:
You’re going to ask if it’s always this easy.

DEACON:
Is it?

KESTREL:
It’s this easy for about three years. Ask Marguerite what year four is like. Actually don’t, she’ll tell you, and then you’ll have to know it.

She rolls off down the hall. Deacon Vox looks at himself in the mirror for a long moment, and then says his own name out loud to see whether it fits yet.

DEACON:
Deacon Vox.

FADE out 3

SLUG THE PRINE HOUSE, THE KITCHEN | 2080, SPRING

A year in. The house is fuller, louder, and better fed. There are now two cameras, which in this business is an empire.

BUCK OZAROW is at the table with a catalogue, a calculator, and the doomed patience of a man building a plan out of a world that does not take plans.

BUCK:
Eleven thousand four hundred. That’s the number.

REED:
That’s the number for what?

BUCK:
For the shop. Buck’s Audio. Two rooms on the Coldwater strip, a bench, a soldering station, and every speaker in the Basin that still works comes through my door.

REED:
Nobody fixes speakers, Buck.

BUCK:
Everybody NEEDS speakers fixed. Nobody fixes them. Do you understand that those are two halves of the same sentence and the sentence is money?

SABLE:
Buck, honey, that’s the smartest thing anyone’s said in this house in eleven months.

BUCK:
Thank you.

SABLE:
It’ll never happen.

BUCK:
Why won’t it happen?

SABLE:
Because nobody lends to us. Look at me. Look at what I do. I have eleven thousand credits in a coffee tin because no bank in the Basin will hold it.

BUCK:
The Meridian Mutual takes anybody. It says so on the sign. It says "we take anybody."

SABLE:
It means anybody with a job they can say out loud.

WAIT 3

SLUG MERIDIAN MUTUAL, LOAN DESK | THE NEXT WEEK

Buck Ozarow, in his best coat, sitting very straight, across from a clerk with a form and a policy and no particular malice.

CLERK:
Occupation.

BUCK:
Broadcast.

CLERK:
Broadcast what.

BUCK:
Broadcast performance.

CLERK:
Mister Ozarow, the form has a box and the box has a word in it. I am going to have to ask you to say the word.

BUCK:
Adult. Adult broadcast. I’m on the tapes.

The clerk stops writing. She does not sneer. It is much worse than a sneer. She simply stops writing, and puts the cap back on the pen, which is a decision.

CLERK:
I see.

BUCK:
It’s legal. There isn’t a law about it. There isn’t a law about anything.

CLERK:
It is not a question of legality, Mister Ozarow. It is a question of character. The lending policy requires character.

BUCK:
I have character. I have eleven thousand credits of character. I have never missed a payment on anything in my life because I have never been ALLOWED to owe anything.

CLERK:
The policy is the policy. I’m sorry.

BUCK:
No you’re not. You’re not sorry, you’re relieved. There’s a difference and I can hear it, I work in sound.

He takes his coat off the back of the chair with enormous dignity and leaves, which is the only thing left to take.

FADE out 2

SLUG THE PRINE HOUSE, THE POOL DECK | SUMMER

Auggie Prine holds court in a drained pool. This is where he does his thinking, because it is the only place in the house with no cable in it.

AUGGIE:
I want to do something and everyone is going to tell me it’s a mistake.

WILBY:
It’s a mistake.

AUGGIE:
You haven’t heard it.

WILBY:
I’ve worked for you for nine years, Auggie. It’s a mistake and I’ll do it.

AUGGIE:
I want to make one that’s ninety minutes. One story. Same people the whole way through. A beginning that you have to sit through to understand the end.

REED:
Who sits still for ninety minutes?

AUGGIE:
Everybody used to! Everybody in the world used to! They used to leave the HOUSE to do it, Reed, they used to pay money and sit in the dark with strangers for two hours and nobody thought that was strange!

REED:
And then the world ended.

AUGGIE:
And then the world ended and we all decided people are only good for four minutes at a time. I don’t accept it. I have never accepted it.

SABLE:
Auggie. Sweetheart. Tell them the real reason.

AUGGIE:
That is the real reason.

SABLE:
Tell them the other real reason.

Auggie Prine is quiet for a moment. In the drained pool his voice does that thing that voices do in a drained pool.

AUGGIE:
Because everybody in the Basin already knows what we sell and everybody in the Basin already gets it for free, and the ONLY thing left to be good at is the ninety minutes around it.

AUGGIE:
And because in forty years there will be nothing left from now. Nothing. Everything we make gets recopied into mud and everything before us is in a corp vault.

AUGGIE:
And if there is going to be exactly one thing left that says people were here and they were trying, I would like it to be ninety minutes long and I would like it to be GOOD.

Nobody says anything for a while. Kestrel takes her headset off. It is the only time in this picture that she does.

KESTREL:
What’s it called?

AUGGIE:
The Open Signal.

FADE out 2

SLUG THE BASIN | 2081

A montage of the good years, which is the part everybody remembers and nobody was paying attention during.

A new mast on the ridge, paid for in cash, wired by four people who did not know what they were doing and did it anyway.

Deacon Vox on a poster on a wall in the Yards, and a queue of people waiting outside a bar to watch a broadcast on a screen the size of a door.

Marguerite Sable teaching Kestrel how to hit a mark. Kestrel hitting the mark. Kestrel hitting the mark better than Marguerite Sable.

Buck Ozarow in a shirt he bought for himself, carrying a speaker cabinet up the stairs of a house that is not his, whistling.

Scotty Kell learning to keep the boom out of frame, mostly, on the eighth try, to genuine applause.

Reed Halloran crying on the count of four, in a wide shot, in the rain, for the fourteenth time, without complaint, because it is the job.

And Deacon Vox in the middle of it, at twenty-one, with money in a shoebox and a family who chose him and a body that has not failed him once.

NARRATOR:
For about three years, in a rented house above a dead city, a group of people who had been thrown away by everything made the best work of their lives.

NARRATOR:
It is important to say that out loud, because of what happens next, and because everyone who tells this story afterwards leaves this part out.

WAIT 4

SLUG THE BASIN BROADCAST AWARDS, COLDWATER CIVIC HALL | 2081, AUTUMN

An awards night thrown by an industry that legally does not exist, in a hall with a hole in the roof, with trophies made from transmitter parts.

It is, without any irony at all, the happiest night of everybody’s life.

AUGGIE:
Best Tape. Best Direction. Best Performance, Male. Three.

WILBY:
Four. They gave one to Scotty.

AUGGIE:
They gave one to SCOTTY?

WILBY:
Best Boom. They invented it tonight. He’s crying in the corridor.

SCOTTY:
I’m not crying.

WILBY:
He’s crying in the corridor.

Marguerite Sable comes off the stage holding a trophy welded out of a broken antenna, and she is not entirely present.

SABLE:
Auggie. Auggie, I have to tell you something and you have to not make a thing of it.

AUGGIE:
Tell me.

SABLE:
I filed for the boy again. My son. The hearing’s in the spring.

AUGGIE:
Marguerite, that’s wonderful.

SABLE:
It’s not wonderful, it’s the fourth time. They ask what I do. Every time they ask what I do and every time I say it and every time I watch them write it down.

AUGGIE:
So say something else.

SABLE:
I’m not ashamed of it.

AUGGIE:
I didn’t say be ashamed of it. I said SAY something else. There is a difference between shame and strategy and one of them gets you your son back.

SABLE:
Auggie, they have watched the tapes. That is the part nobody says. Somebody in that building has sat down and watched me work in order to decide whether I am fit, and then gone home to his family.

AUGGIE:
Marguerite.

SABLE:
He’s eleven. He lives in an arcology creche in a building I am not allowed into. He has a number. My son has a NUMBER, Auggie.

She stops. She puts the trophy down on a table and does not pick it up again, and later somebody else will take it home by accident.

Across the room, Deacon Vox is accepting Best Performance, and he has prepared nothing, and it does not matter.

DEACON:
I don’t... okay. Two years ago I washed plates at the Meridian and a man came out to the lot and told me I had something.

DEACON:
And everybody in this room has been told their whole life that what they’ve got is the wrong thing to have. That it’s a joke, or it’s dirty, or it’s an accident.

DEACON:
It’s not an accident. It’s the only card I got dealt and I have worked it like a trade for two years, and there is not one person out there who can do what we do in this room.

The room comes apart. Two hundred people who have all been told they are nothing, in a hall with a hole in the roof, at the exact top of their lives.

Auggie Prine watches from the back with his hands in his pockets, and he is not smiling, because he is a director and he has seen the shape of this scene before and he knows what act it comes in.

FADE out 3

ACT 2: New Year

SLUG THE PRINE HOUSE | NEW YEAR’S EVE, 2081

Every light in the house is on. There are ninety people here and the host knows about forty of them.

In the kitchen, a man nobody invited is telling everybody about a business opportunity. This is TODD VANE.

TODD:
It’s not a scam, that’s the thing, everybody hears the structure and says scam, and I say, name me one thing that isn’t.

REED:
The structure IS the scam, Todd.

TODD:
The structure is a STRUCTURE, Reed. You put money in the top, and it comes out the bottom, and it comes out of the bottom SOMEWHERE.

REED:
Where?

TODD:
Not our problem! That’s the beauty!

DEACON:
Who is this guy?

REED:
That’s Todd. Todd Vane. He came with someone. He always came with someone.

DEACON:
He’s got a gun in his coat.

REED:
Yeah. Yeah, he does. Everybody’s decided not to bring that up tonight.

Out by the pool, WILBY CRANE stands alone with a drink he is not drinking, listening to the house.

Because everybody in this picture knows what Wilby’s wife does at parties, and everybody in this picture has decided, for nine years, that it is not their business.

Inside, in the front room, in front of eleven people, VAL CRANE is on the floor with a man nobody knows, and she is not being quiet about it.

VAL:
Wilby! Wilby, come and look at this!

WILBY:
Val.

VAL:
Come and LOOK, Wilby, come and stand right there where you always stand!

WILBY:
Val, there’s people here.

VAL:
There’s ALWAYS people here! That’s the arrangement! That was the arrangement you agreed to because you never once said no to anything in your life!

WILBY:
I’d like you to come upstairs now.

VAL:
Say it louder, sweetheart. The sound man can’t get his levels.

The eleven people in the front room look at the floor, at the ceiling, at their drinks. Nobody helps. Nobody has ever helped.

Wilby Crane goes out to the pool and stands in the dark and does not do anything, which is the thing he has done for nine years and which is the reason for everything that follows.

SABLE:
Wilby. Honey. Come inside.

WILBY:
It’s a nice night.

SABLE:
It’s freezing.

WILBY:
It’s a nice night, Marguerite.

SABLE:
Wilby, do you want me to say something to her? I’ll say something to her, I don’t care.

WILBY:
Don’t say anything to her. She’s not doing anything I didn’t let her do.

SABLE:
That’s not the same as fine.

WILBY:
No. No, it isn’t.

He looks at his watch. There are nine minutes left in the year.

