The eleven people in the front room look at the floor, at the ceiling, at their drinks. Nobody helps. Nobody has ever helped.

A woman by the door says, quietly, to nobody, that somebody should do something, and then finishes her drink instead, and that is the closest anybody comes.

Wilby Crane comes back inside from the pool. He is very calm. He has the particular calm of a man who has stopped having a decision to make.

He walks past Auggie. He walks past Reed, who says something to him that he does not hear.

He goes up the stairs.

REED:
Wilby! Hey, Wilby, they’re counting!

The house begins to count.

SCOTTY:
Ten! Nine!

REED:
Eight! Seven!

SABLE:
Six! Five!

KESTREL:
Four.

The gunshot is not loud. That is the thing nobody who tells this story afterwards can ever get across. In a house with ninety people and a sound system, it is not loud at all.

The second one is not loud either.

For four full seconds, the party keeps counting, because a party has momentum and the news has not arrived yet.

DEACON:
Three.

TODD:
Two!

Then the third one, from upstairs, and that one everybody hears, because the house has gone quiet enough to hear it.

