SLUG THE PRINE HOUSE, THE LANDING | MIDNIGHT

Ninety people leave a house in under three minutes without speaking. It is the fastest and quietest anybody in the Basin has ever moved.

At the top of the stairs, Auggie Prine stands in a doorway with his hand on the frame, not going in.

AUGGIE:
Don’t come up here.

SABLE:
Auggie...

AUGGIE:
Marguerite, do not come up here. I am asking you as your friend and I am telling you as the man who owns this house.

SABLE:
Is she...

AUGGIE:
Both of them. And him. All three.

Downstairs, one radio in the kitchen is still playing something bright, because nobody has thought to turn it off, and it plays for another eleven minutes.

Deacon Vox stands in the hall in a good coat, twenty-one years old, at the exact top of his life, with his ears ringing.

DEACON:
Auggie. What do we do.

AUGGIE:
We shoot on Tuesday.

DEACON:
Auggie.

AUGGIE:
We shoot on Tuesday, Deacon. That is what we do. If we do not shoot on Tuesday then this is the last thing that ever happened to any of us and I will not have that be true.

The camera holds on the landing. Nobody moves. The bright music keeps playing downstairs.

FADE out 5

SLUG A ROADHOUSE ON THE COAST ROAD | JANUARY, 2082

Two weeks later. Auggie Prine meets a man called FISK GONDOLLI, who has come up from the Coast with a case, and who is smiling.

GONDOLLI:
Auggie. My condolences. Genuinely. Terrible thing.

AUGGIE:
Thank you.

GONDOLLI:
Terrible, terrible thing. Now. Let me show you what’s going to happen to your business.

AUGGIE:
That’s a hell of a segue, Fisk.

GONDOLLI:
I’m a busy man and you’re a grieving one, so let’s save each other the middle part.

He opens the case. Inside is a feed rig: small, ugly, cheap, and it does not use tape.

GONDOLLI:
No stock. No developing. No lab. It writes straight to a chip and the chip copies itself, forever, for nothing.

AUGGIE:
It looks like garbage.

GONDOLLI:
It IS garbage. Look at it. The picture is soft, the colour’s a lie, and it’ll never look like your tape. Not in a hundred years.

AUGGIE:
Then why am I sitting here?

GONDOLLI:
Because it costs eleven credits a broadcast and yours costs nine hundred, and because nobody out there can tell the difference, and Auggie, this is the part I need you to actually hear, nobody out there WANTS to.

AUGGIE:
People know the difference.

GONDOLLI:
People know the difference between a good meal and a bad one and they still eat what’s in front of them.

AUGGIE:
My work looks like something.

GONDOLLI:
Your work looks BEAUTIFUL and it takes you four months and it costs a fortune and in two years you will be the last man in the Basin still doing it and they will call you an artist, which is what they call a man they have stopped paying.

AUGGIE:
And what would you have me do instead?

GONDOLLI:
Ten a month. Twenty a month. Forty minutes each, shot in a day, no story, no lighting, no rehearsal. Just the kid and a room.

AUGGIE:
