INTERMISSION 45

ACT 3: The Reclamation

SLUG COLDWATER, THE LICENSING NOTICE | 2082, SPRING

The corps did not raid the Basin. Nobody kicked a door. What happened was a notice, printed on good paper, nailed to the door of the civic hall.

NARRATOR:
By order of the Reclamation Authority, the broadcast spectrum of the Coldwater Basin is hereby reassigned to licensed operators.

NARRATOR:
Unlicensed transmission is a Class Two infraction. Licences are available. The fee is forty thousand credits per mast per year, payable in advance, and the character requirements are attached.

NARRATOR:
It took the Reclamation Authority one afternoon and one clerk to end eight years, and neither the afternoon nor the clerk has ever been named in a history.

SLUG THE PRINE HOUSE, THE LOUNGE | THAT WEEK

The house, in daylight, with the lamps off. It looks smaller with the lamps off. Everything does.

AUGGIE:
Forty thousand.

COLONEL:
Per mast. Per year.

AUGGIE:
We don’t have forty thousand.

COLONEL:
We have forty thousand.

AUGGIE:
Then pay it.

COLONEL:
Auggie, the character requirements are attached. Do you understand what that phrase is doing at the bottom of that page?

AUGGIE:
Say it plainly.

COLONEL:
They will not licence a man who makes what you make. Not for forty thousand and not for four hundred. The fee is not the wall. The fee is the door in the wall, and we are not the ones it’s for.

AUGGIE:
So we don’t licence.

COLONEL:
So we don’t licence, and every mast in the Basin gets an inspector, and the inspectors are already hired, and I have met two of them.

AUGGIE:
Then what are you telling me, Colonel? Say the actual sentence.

COLONEL:
I’m telling you Fisk Gondolli has a licence, on the Coast, in a bonded warehouse, with forty feed rigs and nothing on them.

AUGGIE:
No.

COLONEL:
And he has asked for you. Not me. You. He wants the Prine name on a licensed feed and he will pay for the privilege.

AUGGIE:
I said no.

COLONEL:
Auggie. I have eleven people living in your house.

AUGGIE:
They’re not living in my house, they’re living in my WORK, there’s a difference and it is the only difference I have left.

