They do not take his money, because he does not have any. They take his coat and his boots, and they break two of his ribs, and one of them keeps saying the name, the stage name, over and over, delighted, like a man who has found a celebrity in a bin.

And the last thing they do, before they go, is tell him exactly what he is, at length, in detail, using the same word his mother would not say in the kitchen five years ago.

Deacon Vox lies in a service alley off the Coldwater strip at three in the morning, in the wet, without a coat, with two ribs gone.

He gets up on the fourth try. He does not cry. He walks east, because east is where the house is, and he does not go there either.

