FADE out 2

SLUG THE PRINE HOUSE | 2085, SPRING

The house from the outside. The lamps are off. There is grass growing through the crack in the pool deck, which the film holds on for a full four seconds because that is the entire second half of this movie in one image.

Deacon Vox comes up the drive.

Marguerite Sable is on the step. She is thinner. She is present. She has been clean for four months and she looks like somebody who is counting them.

SABLE:
Well.

DEACON:
Marguerite.

SABLE:
Come here.

She holds on to him for a very long time. Neither of them says anything about anything.

SABLE:
He’s round the back.

DEACON:
Is he going to throw me out?

SABLE:
Probably. He throws everybody out. He threw ME out in October and I came back in November and neither of us has mentioned it.

DEACON:
Marguerite. Your son.

SABLE:
Twelve visits a year. Supervised. I got it in February.

DEACON:
That’s...

SABLE:
It’s twelve. It’s an hour each. It’s the best thing that has ever happened to me and I would like you to not say anything else about it, because I will come apart and I have got a schedule.

WAIT 2

SLUG THE PRINE HOUSE, THE DRAINED POOL | CONTINUOUS

Auggie Prine, in a deck chair, in a swimming pool with no water in it, with nothing to direct.

He does not get up. He does not turn around. He has heard the footsteps and he knows the walk, because he has been looking at people professionally for fifteen years.

AUGGIE:
You’re late.

DEACON:
Auggie.

AUGGIE:
Two years late.

DEACON:
I know.

AUGGIE:
Sit down. There’s a chair. It’s a terrible chair, everything here is terrible now, sit in it.

Deacon Vox sits down in a drained swimming pool next to the man who came out to a roadhouse lot for him.

AUGGIE:
Are you working?

DEACON:
No.

AUGGIE:
Can you?

DEACON:
Auggie, I’m not going to...

AUGGIE:
I am not asking as a man with a camera. I have not had a camera in eleven months. I am asking because you walked up my drive and you have not looked at me once.

A long pause in a concrete hole above a dead city.

DEACON:
Sometimes.

AUGGIE:
Sometimes is a career. Sometimes is most people’s whole career.

DEACON:
It isn’t mine. Mine was the one that always worked.

AUGGIE:
Yes. And that is the worst thing that ever happened to you, and I gave it to you, and I have had two years in this pool to think about that.

DEACON:
Don’t.

AUGGIE:
I found a boy in a lot and I told him the thing about him that everybody had been whispering, and I said it kindly, and I meant it kindly, and I have watched what saying it kindly did.

DEACON:
I said yes.

AUGGIE:
You were nineteen and you were holding a crate.

DEACON:
I said YES, Auggie. I have said yes every single day since. You do not get to take that off me as well. It’s the only thing in the whole five years I actually did.

Auggie Prine looks at him for a long moment, and then does something he has not done in the entire picture, which is agree.

AUGGIE:
Alright.

DEACON:
Alright?

AUGGIE:
Alright. You said yes. It’s yours. All of it, the whole five years, the good and the rest. It’s yours and you did it.

They sit in the pool. Somewhere in the house a door goes and Kestrel comes out onto the deck with a broken headset around her neck that she has never replaced.

KESTREL:
Hi.

DEACON:
Hi.

KESTREL:
You look terrible.

DEACON:
You too.

KESTREL:
Yeah.

She sits down on the edge of the pool with her feet over the side, and that is all, and it is enough, because these people have never once been able to say the thing.

AUGGIE:
I want to shoot something.

DEACON:
There’s no licence, Auggie. There’s no mast.

AUGGIE:
There is a mast on the ridge that nobody has climbed in fourteen months and there is an inspector in Coldwater who is paid to inspect the Basin, and I have met him, and he is one man with a truck.

KESTREL:
That’s not a plan, that’s a grievance with a map.

AUGGIE:
It is ninety minutes, is what it is. One story. Same people the whole way through.

DEACON:
Auggie. I can’t promise you I can do it.

AUGGIE:
I did not ask you to promise me anything. I asked you to stand on a mark in a house you know. That is all I have ever asked anybody in fifteen years.

DEACON:
And if it doesn’t happen?

AUGGIE:
Then it doesn’t happen, and we go again after lunch, and nobody in this house says a word about it. Nobody in this house has ever said a word about it.

Deacon Vox puts his head back against a concrete wall in a drained swimming pool and laughs, once, badly, because his ribs are still wrong.

FADE out 3

SLUG THE PRINE HOUSE, THE LOUNGE SET | LATER

The lamps go on one at a time. Three of them. It takes a while, because two are broken and Buck Ozarow has to be called.

Reed Halloran arrives in a security uniform he does not change out of, and hits his mark, and is home.

Scotty Kell comes off a haul route with a boom pole in the cab of a truck that does not belong to him, and nobody asks.

Marguerite Sable puts a light where a light should be. Kestrel stands where a camera should be and finds she can see the whole room from there.

AUGGIE:
Quiet, please.

The house goes quiet, the way it used to, the way rooms do when they have agreed to take something seriously.

AUGGIE:
Speed.

There is no sound man. Auggie Prine says "speed" to nobody, because that is the word, and the word is the job, and the job is what is left.

AUGGIE:
And ACTION.

LETTERBOX off

FADE in 3

SLUG THE PRINE HOUSE, DEACON’S ROOM | MOMENTS BEFORE

The very last shot of the picture is not the set. The picture cuts backwards, three minutes, to a man alone in a room with a mirror.

Deacon Vox, twenty-five, in the room he lived in at nineteen, in front of the same full-length mirror, on the night it matters.

He is not warming up. There is nothing to warm up. He is doing the thing he has done in front of this mirror ten thousand times and has not been able to do for a year.

He opens his robe.

The film, which has spent two and a half hours pointing the camera at everybody else’s face: at Auggie in the lot, at the crew at the back of the room, at eleven people looking at the ceiling, finally, in its last thirty seconds, looks.

And there it is. The one card. The accident in the womb. The reason for the house, the name, the money, the awards, the mother, the alley, all of it.

It is not a punchline and the film does not play it as one. It is just true, the way it has always been true, and it has cost him everything he had.

Deacon Vox looks at himself in the mirror for a long moment.

DEACON:
Alright.

DEACON:
Alright. Okay. There you are.

He closes the robe. He puts his hands on the dresser and breathes for a second like a man about to lift something.

And then he says the name, the one Kestrel gave him at a kitchen table when he was nineteen and everybody said it out loud until it stuck.

DEACON:
I’m Deacon Vox.

He goes downstairs, where three lamps are on and a man with no sound crew is saying "speed" to nobody, because that is the word, and the word is the job, and the job is what is left.

CUT TO BLACK.

FADE out 4

